// Getting started: a VM from a registry template, its console, screen and snapshots.
page gs_first_vm {
  title = "Your first VM"
  chapter_header "Your first VM" {
    kicker       = "Getting started · tutorial"
    reading_time = "9 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    In this tutorial you run a full virtual machine without building
    anything. The VM's `template` field names a template published on a
    public OCI registry, and `vmlab up` pulls it into your local store the
    first time. You then open a shell in the guest, attach its console, take
    a screenshot, and take and restore a snapshot.
  DOC

  h2 "Before you start"

  list {
    li "vmlab is installed with the runtime tools from [Install](gs_install). No guest assets are needed: the pulled template already carries the agent."
    li "`/dev/kvm` is available. The template is x86_64, so it runs under KVM on an x86_64 host."
    li "Internet access from the host, to pull from `ghcr.io`. The template is small, and the pull takes a few tens of seconds."
    li "A VNC viewer for the console step. `remote-viewer` from `virt-viewer` is the one vmlab prefers. The step also shows the path with no viewer at all."
  }

  h2 "Write the lab file"

  p "Create a new directory with this `vmlab.wcl`."

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab.wcl>

lab "first-vm" {

  vm "alp" {
    template = "ghcr.io/vmlabdev/vmlab-templates/alpine-3.23"
    arch     = "x86_64"
    memory   = 1GiB
    nic { nat = true }
  }
}
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `template` is an OCI registry reference of the form
    `host/owner/[group/]name[:tag]`. With no tag it tracks the moving
    `latest` tag, the newest stable version of the published template.
    `:latest-prerelease` tracks pre-releases and `:<version>` pins one.
    A registry reference always needs `arch`, because one tag can carry
    several architectures and vmlab never assumes the host's.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    `nic { nat = true }` is shorthand for attaching the VM to the lab's
    built-in NAT segment, so you do not have to declare one. The guest gets a
    DHCP lease and internet egress. `memory = 1GiB` overrides the size the
    template recorded; `cpus` and everything else are inherited from the
    template and, below it, from its guest OS profile. See
    [Lab file: vm and its children](ref_vm) for the whole surface.
  DOC

  h2 "Bring it up"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab validate
vmlab up
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `up` resolves the tag, finds no matching version in the local store,
    pulls the template, installs it under `x86_64/alpine-3.23@<version>`, and
    creates the VM as a linked clone of it. The clone is a qcow2 file whose
    backing file is the sealed template, so it starts small and the template
    is never modified. The VM boots and `up` waits until the guest agent
    answers, which is what **ready** means.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A cached version is reused on every later `up` and never re-pulled
    implicitly. To fetch a template without starting anything, for instance
    ahead of a trip, run `vmlab pull`. To see what the store holds, list it.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab status
vmlab template list
SRC
  }

  callout "The VM is running" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`vmlab status` shows `alp` running and ready with an address. `vmlab template list` shows `x86_64/alpine-3.23` with the version that was pulled."
  }

  h2 "Open a shell"

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab shell` attaches an interactive terminal inside the guest over the
    agent's virtio-serial channel. No SSH and no guest network are involved,
    so it works even on a VM with no NIC. This VM declares no `login {}`, so
    the shell runs as the agent's identity, which is root on Linux.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab shell alp" }

  p "Inside the guest, confirm where you are, then detach with Ctrl-]. Detaching leaves the VM running."

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
cat /etc/alpine-release
ip addr show eth0
SRC
  }

  p "For a single command, use `exec` instead. Its exit code becomes the exit code of `vmlab exec`."

  code sh { source = "vmlab exec alp -- uname -a" }

  h2 "Attach the console"

  p <<DOC
    Every VM has a VNC display served on a unix socket whether or not anyone
    is looking at it. `vmlab console` attaches a viewer to it. vmlab picks the
    viewer from your host configuration, else the first of `remote-viewer`,
    `gvncviewer` and `vncviewer` on your `PATH`. Closing the viewer window
    only disconnects. The VM keeps running.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab console alp" }

  p <<DOC
    You see the Alpine login prompt on the virtual screen. With no viewer
    installed, or on WSL 2 where the viewer lives on the Windows side, ask
    for a TCP bridge instead. vmlab forwards the display to a localhost port
    and prints the address for you to point any VNC client at.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab console --tcp alp" }

  p <<DOC
    To open a viewer automatically on every `up`, set `gui = true` on the VM
    or on the lab. The VM still runs headless; the viewer is a separate client
    process.
  DOC

  h2 "Take a screenshot"

  p <<DOC
    The same display that the console shows can be captured to a PNG at any
    moment. This is the basis of screen-driven automation, where a script
    waits for an image or a piece of text to appear.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab vm screenshot alp login.png" }

  p <<DOC
    Open `login.png` and you see the same login prompt. With `tesseract`
    installed, `vmlab vm ocr alp` reads the text off the screen instead, and
    `vmlab vm sendkeys alp <chord>` types into it. [Screens, input and
    vision](guide_vision) covers the whole surface.
  DOC

  callout "You have three doors into the guest" {
    kind = :success
    body = "You have run a command over the agent, watched the screen over VNC, and captured it to a file."
  }

  h2 "Snapshot and restore"

  p <<DOC
    A [snapshot](guide_snapshots) records a machine's disk, and if the machine
    is running, its RAM and device state too. Every snapshot remembers the
    power state it was taken in. Restoring an online snapshot resumes the
    guest exactly where it was; restoring an offline one leaves it powered
    off. Write a marker file in the guest, take a snapshot, delete the marker,
    then restore.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab exec alp -- sh -c 'echo before > /root/marker'
vmlab snapshot create clean --vm alp
vmlab snapshot list alp
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `--vm alp` narrows the snapshot to one machine. Without it, `snapshot
    create` captures every VM and container in the lab under one name.
    Consistency across machines in a lab-wide snapshot is best-effort, not
    coordinated. Now change the guest and roll it back.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab exec alp -- rm /root/marker
vmlab snapshot restore clean --vm alp
vmlab exec alp -- cat /root/marker
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    The last command prints `before`. The VM was running when the snapshot
    was taken, so it is running again after the restore, with the marker
    back. Delete the snapshot when you no longer need it.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab snapshot delete alp clean" }

  callout "Snapshots are not a workspace backup" {
    kind = :note
    body = "On a dev machine the workspace is re-converged from the host after a restore rather than rolled back with the disk. The source of truth for a workspace is the host directory. See [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev)."
  }

  h2 "Clean up"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab down
vmlab destroy
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `destroy` deletes the clone and the lab-local state. The pulled template
    stays in the store, so the next `up` of any lab that names it starts
    without a download. To remove it, find its version with `vmlab template
    list` and remove that reference.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab template rm x86_64/alpine-3.23@<version>" }

  h2 "Next steps"

  list {
    li "[Build a template from an ISO](gs_first_template) makes your own template from installer media, so any operating system can be a lab machine."
    li "[Distributing templates over registries](guide_registries) explains tags, multi-arch indexes, pushing your own templates and searching a registry."
    li "[Snapshots](guide_snapshots) explains online and offline snapshots, what a lab-wide snapshot guarantees, and how snapshots interact with shared folders."
    li "[vmlab vm](cmd_vm), [vmlab snapshot](cmd_snapshot) and [vmlab console](cmd_console) document the verbs used here."
  }
}
